reading the table:
- alloc/window P is the INGESTION path (feed -> fold -> frame): the number the
  "no GC scan overhead" claim is about. Mode A (reactive model) pays ~900 KB per
  100 ticks; B and D (the same SoA engine through pooled / hand-rolled transport)
  measure at sampling noise (144 B).
- Mode C produce: 144 B/window — IDENTICAL to B and D. History: this row
  once measured 8.9 KB/window, attributed by subtraction to the kernel TS
  port's per-publish advisory telemetry — BigInt atomics (t_publish, t_wsteps,
  BigInt(seq) canary) at ~90 B/publish, a declared TS-port cost. The 2026-09-16
  telemetry regime change in core/ts/weft.ts (dual-i32 halves, Number step
  counters, dual-u32 canary) CLOSED that gap to the sampling-noise floor:
  the microbench run beside this log measured ~152 B → ~0.3 B per
  publish+claim cycle and a ~1.5x throughput improvement. P+C once added the
  consume path on top: one rReadSlice view object per claim (W6 runner) or
  two view objects per frame (W1-W5 ModeCRunner: rReadSlice + a fresh
  Float32Array wrapper) — ~20.9 KB/window of runner-side allocation. The
  2026-09-16 consume-path fix closed it: both runners now claim once and
  read through rLiveFloat32() — the kernel's per-slot CACHED live view,
  the documented draw-phase API (TS analog of C's weft_r_live_ptr).
  P+C now measures 144 B/window for ALL modes: B == C == D at the sampling-
  noise floor end-to-end — produce AND consume, Law 2 closed through the
  whole frame path.
- GC ev / GC pause: Mode A logged 15 collection events and ~2 ms of pause over
  3,000 ticks at DEFAULT heap settings; B/C/D logged 0. A larger nursery defers
  scavenge timing without changing the allocation-rate column — event counts
  are lower bounds on pressure, and the P100 tail tells the same story
  (A: ~260 µs worst tick vs C: ~19 µs).
- leak (KB) near zero for every path: no path LEAKS — the difference is garbage
  production rate, which is what drives collection pauses at real feed scales.

